id: recipe.event-ingestion
kind: recipe
title: Event ingestion
aliases: event ingestion, ingest events, telemetry ingest, 100k events per minute
question: how would you design a service receiving 100k events per minute?
tags: architecture, scale, streams
properties:
  - sustained throughput with bounded memory
  - ordered per key, unordered globally
  - durable before acknowledged
  - idempotent downstream effects
invariant: Persist before acknowledging, partition by key for ordering, and bound every hop so that backpressure reaches the producer.
answer_30s: A thin ingest tier that validates and appends to a partitioned durable log (batched writes), consumers that process per partition with bounded concurrency, and idempotent sink writes keyed by event id.
flow: |
  producers -> Ingress/API (validate, authenticate)
     |
  batch append -> durable log / queue (partitioned by key)
     |
  consumers per partition (bounded concurrency, ordered per key)
     |
  sinks: database / warehouse / cache (idempotent by event id)
     |
  backlog per partition = the scaling signal
compose:
  - design.producer-consumer => ingest and processing rates decouple
  - design.exactly-once => at-least-once delivery plus dedup by event id
  - design.idempotency => replays and consumer retries are safe
  - design.bounded-queue => per-partition backlog bounded and observable
  - design.backpressure => slow sinks slow ingestion instead of growing memory
  - design.worker-pool => consumer concurrency is a chosen number per partition
  - design.observability => ingest rate, per-partition lag, sink latency, dedup rate
  - design.timeouts => sink writes bounded so a slow sink does not stall a partition
  - k8s.deployment => consumers scale with lag
  - k8s.hpa => scale on lag, not CPU
  - rust.tokio-mpsc => bounded in-process buffering
  - rust.bounded-concurrency => batched sink writes with a concurrency cap
failure_modes:
  - acknowledging before persistence, so a crash loses accepted events
  - a single partition or single consumer, so throughput is capped at one core
  - unbounded in-memory buffering between tiers, so a sink outage becomes an OOM
  - no dedup on replay, so a reprocessed window duplicates rows
follow_ups:
  - a sink is down for two hours: what happens to the backlog and to memory?
related:
  - design.producer-consumer
  - design.exactly-once
  - recipe.high-throughput-telemetry
  - design.telemetry-pipeline
sources:
  - https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#design
